--- body ---

## Context

Author request: "Ticket that. Sweeping every 30s is dumb. But what if a file
disappear from disk for example ?" The event-driven direction is ratified in
DECISIONS.md (2026-09-08); SPEC.md §5.2.7 owns the scheduling contract and its
reconciliation cadence.

Read before filing: plugins/sdt-sitter/bootstrap.js and scheduler.js still run
repeated full censuses; fork/src has no SDT sitter implementation. SYNC.md's
Zotero core/extraction rows distinguish native extraction from the external
consumer. This is local plugin work, not an upstream filing.

## Work

Subscribe to appropriate Zotero notifications and inspect affected attachment
IDs through a coalesced queue. Verify the actual notification contract, including
attachment downloads and deletion, against the installed host; do not assume
every disk change produces an item event. Events must not each launch a full
census. Retain startup and occasional reconciliation for unnotified changes.
Implement the subsequently ratified reconciliation cadence in SPEC.md;
distinguish queue/resource retries from full censuses. Coalesce missed intervals
without overlapping scans, and reconcile when re-enabled.

File disappearance is an availability change, not deletion of the Zotero item.
On observation, report the source as missing, remove obsolete queued work and
stop counting it as available/current merely because an old pack remains. Do
not delete the library item or its native pack. Recheck availability before
admission and handle disappearance during extraction without a retry storm.
A restored/downloaded file must become eligible again after inspection;
unnotified restoration must be found by reconciliation. Preserve the ruled
session-scoped extraction-failure policy and source-identity invalidation.

## Exit criteria

- [x] Verified notification mapping and lifecycle cleanup; repeated events
      coalesce and changes arriving during inspection/extraction are not lost.
- [x] Initial reconciliation and ordinary event processing replace the recurring
      full-census loop; quiet-library checks prove events do not trigger sweeps.
- [x] Reconciliation detects source disappearance/restoration, external source
      replacement, pack deletion and processor changes without notifications.
- [x] Source disappearance before admission and during extraction has an honest
      outcome; restoring a missing file is not trapped behind an obsolete status.
- [x] UI preserves coherent coverage and exposes last reconciliation freshness;
      it does not present last-observed disk availability as continuously verified.
- [x] On/off, disable/re-enable, resource waits, single native admission and
      unresolved-job handling retain SPEC.md §5.2.7 semantics.
- [x] Behavioral tests cover the above races and missed-event recovery; evidence
      is committed under verification/ and cited here. Documentation is updated.
- [x] Ratified reconciliation policy implemented and make check passes.

## Raid plan and feasibility

Read-only Imagine/Plan review: feasible as a scheduler refactor, not merely a
replacement timer. Keep a status map, coalesced dirty attachment IDs and a pending
reconciliation flag behind one serial pump. Events arriving during awaited work
must remain pending. Reconciliation and targeted updates share inspection but
only a completed full census may prune the derived cache against its seen set.
Keep admission retries separate from discovery; recheck source identity and
availability before submission and after a failed extraction. Verified current
packs must not be hidden by an earlier session failure. Refresh processor
identity at reconciliation, not solely at initial activation.

First behavioral test: after an initial census, deliver repeated attachment
notifications while admission is resource-blocked; assert only affected IDs are
inspected, list() is not repeated, and work resumes after the resource clears.
Then drive events during inspection/extraction, missed reconciliation intervals,
parent/child deletion and restoration, lifecycle races, and the unnotified disk
changes in the exit criteria. Preserve the existing mutation probes' behavioral
purpose when changing their literal anchors.

Relevant implementation: plugins/sdt-sitter/scheduler.js and bootstrap.js;
tests/sdt_sitter_scheduler.mjs and sdt_sitter_bootstrap.mjs; corresponding
verification/probes/sdt_sitter_*_mutants.py. No added package is needed for native
notifications, Maps/Sets and host timers. Verify installed Zotero notification
surfaces before relying on a particular event type.

